  "worked_solution": "An elementary matrix is a matrix obtained from the identity matrix by applying exactly one elementary row operation. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our matrices.\n\n- The matrix $A$ is an elementary matrix because it can be obtained from ${I}_{2}$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{2}:={R}_{1}+{R}_{2},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & {R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+{R}_{2} \\\\  & \u223c[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The matrix $B$ is an elementary matrix because it can be obtained from ${I}_{2}$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{1}\u2194{R}_{2},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & {R}_{1}\u2194{R}_{2} \\\\  & \u223c[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =B\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The matrix $C$ is not an elementary matrix because it can't be obtained from ${I}_{2}$ by applying only one elementary row operation.\n\nTherefore, only $A$ and $B$ are elementary matrices.",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
